    "question": "CJ, KJ, and AJ collect stamps.  CJ has 5 more than twice the number of stamps that KJ has, and KJ has half as many as AJ.  If the three boys have 930 stamps all together, how many stamps does AJ have?",
    "gold": "370",
    "answer": "Let x represent the number of stamps for AJ\nKJ:x/2 stamps\nCJ:5+2(x/2)=5+x\nTotal:x+(x/2)+5+x=930\n(5/2)x+5=930\n(5/2)x=925\nx=925(2/5)=370 stamps\n#### 370"
  },
  "generation": {
    "text": "Let's define the number of stamps each boy has. Let \\( K \\) be the number of stamps KJ has. According to the problem, KJ has half as many stamps as AJ, so AJ has \\( 2K \\) stamps. Additionally, CJ has 5 more than twice the number of stamps that KJ has, so CJ has \\( 2K + 5 \\) stamps.\n\nThe total number of stamps the three boys have together is 930. Therefore, we can write the equation:\n\\[\nK + 2K + (2K + 5) = 930\n\\]\nSimplifying the left side of the equation, we get:\n\\[\n5K + 5 = 930\n\\]\nNext, we isolate \\( K \\) by subtracting 5 from both sides of the equation:\n\\[\n5K = 925\n\\]\nThen, we solve for \\( K \\) by dividing both sides by 5:\n\\[\nK = 185\n\\]\nNow that we know \\( K = 185 \\), we can find the number of stamps AJ has. Since AJ has \\( 2K \\) stamps, we substitute \\( K = 185 \\) into the expression for AJ:\n\\[\n2K = 2 \\times 185 = 370\n\\]\nTherefore, AJ has \\(\\boxed{370}\\) stamps.",
